insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('643af953-d4ff-5659-abee-7640461efab4'::uuid,'aec20d50-301b-57ce-87d4-64154502a0ef'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'**CHAPTER 7**\n\nMONITORING, EVALUATION, KNOWLEDGE MANAGEMENT AND CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)**\n\n**AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft*\n\n*Subject to institutional, technical, legal and data-privacy validation*\n\n**Draft 1.0 \\| 12 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# Document Control\n\n**Table 7.1**\n\n*Document Control*\n\n| **Document attribute**     | **Control information**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Document title**         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 7                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Chapter title**          | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Position in the Manual** | Seventh of ten chapters in the structure adopted on 12 November 2026. It follows Chapter 6 (DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability) and precedes Chapter 8 (Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships). It receives the reports, after-action records and corrective actions of Chapters 1 to 6 and returns lessons to all chapters. |\n| **Document status**        | Working draft for technical, institutional, legal and data-privacy validation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| **Document owner**         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| **Technical custodian**    | CDRRMO Head; Research and Planning Division as the proposed monitoring and evaluation (M&E) unit                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Reviewing offices**      | City Planning and Development Office (CPDO); City Legal Office; City Data Protection Officer (DPO); CDRRMP Technical Working Group thematic focal offices (CENRO, DILG City Office, CSWDO, CPDO); City Information and Communications Technology office; City Records Officer; Internal Audit Service, where constituted; OCD Region VIII                        |\n| **Version / date**         | Draft 1.0 / 12 November 2026                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| **Approval authority**     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements; in MODE, only the CDRRMO Head approves Manual versions                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| **Review cycle**           | At least annually, after the IAT cycle closes; after a major event or exercise whose AAR changes this chapter; when the NDRRMC issues the RA 12287 monitoring and evaluation framework                                                                                                                                                                           |\n\n## Revision and Approval Record\n\n**Table 7.2**\n\n*Revision and Approval Record*\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | **Prepared / reviewed by**        | **Approval** |\n|-------------|-------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | 12 Nov 2026 | Initial assessment-informed working draft under the ten-chapter structure. Built from IAT v1.7 rows A10.3, A10.4, A10.6, A10.8, A10.9, A5.4, A5.5, B7.3 and B7.4, and from the results-based M&E design of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032. Operates the CAPA register defined in Chapter 1, section 9.5 (GOV-10). Nine SOPs (7-01 to 7-09), nine ME forms, Annexes 7-A to 7-E. | Project drafting team with CDRRMO | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nChapters 1 to 6 set what the City does before, during and after a disaster and how it pays for it. This chapter asks whether those things are being done, whether they work, and what changes as a result. It is the "Check" and "Act" of the Plan–Do–Check–Act cycle that Chapter 1 (section 9) adopts for the whole Manual.\n\nA procedure that is written but never measured drifts. The Introduction (section 5.4) names the risk: with Mandatory compliance at 32.9%, the working standard can settle at "we responded" rather than "we met the standard". The remedy is a fixed standard, measured the same way every year, with gaps tracked to closure and lessons fed back into plans and the Manual. This chapter supplies those mechanisms.\n\nThe IAT v1.7 baseline shows that the City plans and reports, but rarely closes the loop (Figure 7.1). Domain A10 (Partnerships, M&E and Accountability) has an index of **1.86 (Developing)**, with 1 of 4 Mandatory items compliant. Regular reporting to OCD Region VIII and the DILG is in place (A10.3 at 3). The gaps are in the learning loop: no 45-day post-declaration report template (A10.8, score 0, High); no annual M&E report of the LDRRMP (A10.4); after-action reviews that rest on the FGD alone (A10.6, B7.4); no disaster event and loss database (A5.4, score 0); and data-protection arrangements for DRRM data not sighted (A5.5) (Table 7.3).\n\n**Figure 7.1**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Scores for Monitoring, Evaluation and Knowledge Indicators*\n\n![Figure 1: 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale](/library/chp7v10/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Scores are copied from the A_CDRRMO and B_EOC sheets of IAT v1.7. The A10 domain index is Σ(score × weight) ÷ Σweight over A10.1 to A10.9 = 26 ÷ 14 = 1.86, banded on the unrounded value. A5.4, A5.5, B7.3 and B7.4 belong to other domains and are shown because this chapter owns their procedures. All rows are pending validation at the workshop of 1–4 December 2026.\n\n**Table 7.3**\n\n*Assessment Findings That Shape This Chapter*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (IAT v1.7)**                                                               | **Score** | **Priority** | **Finding (summarised from the workbook)**                                                                                            |\n|--------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A10.8  | Post-declaration report within 45 days of lifting a State of Imminent Disaster (M, w2) | 0         | High (12.0)  | No 45-day report template; reporting route not confirmed with OCD VIII. New Mandatory item under RA 12287 IRR Rule VI Sec. 1.         |\n| A5.4   | DRRM information system with disaster event history, damage and losses (GP, w2)        | 0         | Medium (8.0) | Event records exist in reports (10-Year Plan, CDRA, RDANA and PDNA reports) but there is no database.                                 |\n| A10.4  | M&E framework for the LDRRMP with indicators and annual review (M, w2)                 | 2         | Medium (6.0) | The 10-Year Plan sets a results-based M&E design, Form 5D and annual reports; no annual report or baseline database was sighted.      |\n| A5.5   | Data privacy compliance for beneficiary and affected-population data (M, w2)           | 2         | Medium (6.0) | No DPO designation, data-sharing agreement or privacy notice sighted for DRRM data.                                                   |\n| B7.3   | Annual exercise program testing EOC activation (M, w2)                                 | 2         | Medium (6.0) | Exercise program and evaluation records to be validated.                                                                              |\n| A10.6  | After-action reviews after significant events, lessons integrated into plans (GP, w2)  | 2         | Medium (4.0) | FGD self-score of 4 lowered to 2 (Methodology §5.2); AAR reports and resulting plan revisions to be validated.                        |\n| B7.4   | After-action review after each activation, with improvement plan tracked (GP, w2)      | 2         | Medium (4.0) | EOC improvement plan tracking not sighted.                                                                                            |\n| A10.3  | Regular reporting to OCD Region VIII, RDRRMC and DILG (M, w2)                          | 3         | Low (3.0)    | In place; lowered from 4 for lack of a test or AAR. Use of the harmonized reporting template to be confirmed.                         |\n| A10.9  | Structured learning from other LGUs and networks, with changes documented (GP, w1)     | 2         | Low (2.0)    | MCR2030 commitment, NRC Resilient LGU partnership and the EDC Adopt-a-City MOA exist; changes resulting from them are not documented. |\n\n*Note.* Priority is the workbook band of (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 (Mandatory) or × 1.0 (Good Practice): High 9 or more, Medium 4 to 8.9, Low below 4. B7.3 is shared with Chapter 9, which runs the exercise program; this chapter evaluates exercises. Annex 7-A gives the full row set with master requirements and recommended actions.\n\n## 1.1 What the Records Show\n\n-   **A results-based M&E design exists.** The DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 (Chapter 5 of the plan) bases its monitoring on the UNDRR Ten Essentials for Making Cities Resilient, overlaid with NDRRMP 2020–2030 outcomes, the DILG capacity development pillars, the Sendai Framework targets and the Regional Development Plan of Region VIII (Figure 7.3). It sets a monitoring tool (Form 5D) and five outputs, including an annual progress report each January, a physical and financial report each December and a mid-term report in 2029, reported to the CDRRMC and the Local Development Council.\n\n-   **Mandates to monitor exist.** RA 10121 Sec. 11(b) tasks the CDRRMC to approve, monitor and evaluate the LDRRMP and to review and test it regularly. EO 2024-032A Sec. 2(a) repeats the task. EO 2022-00020 created the CDRRMP Technical Working Group for formulating, implementing, monitoring and evaluating the plan, with thematic focal offices: CENRO for prevention and mitigation, the DILG City Office for preparedness, the CSWDO for response and the CPDO for recovery. EO 2023-0026 tasks the Project TRANSFORM TWG to monitor indicators in the NRC resilience scorecard.\n\n-   **The loop is not yet closed.** No annual results report and no baseline database were found in the document set. The plan itself observes that the City''s performance has been assessed mainly through national award systems, without outcome-based M&E linked to sector results.\n\n-   **Event history is scattered.** The 10-Year Plan lists the six most significant disasters; the CDRA lists previous disasters with damage figures; RDANA and PDNA reports exist for 2017 to 2021. The CDRA recommends a disaster event and loss database. Chapter 5 found one damage figure in the plan that is not plausible (Yolanda), which a controlled database would have caught.\n\n-   **The assessment itself is the first full baseline.** The October–November 2026 IAT cycle scored 140 indicators, applied the evidence rule and produced the first Institutional Maturity Index (IMI 1.98). The validation workshop of 1–4 December 2026 will confirm or revise each score. That cycle is the model for the annual cycles this chapter sets.\n\n## 1.2 Design Considerations\n\n1.  **Measure the same way every year.** The IAT, the LDRRMP indicators and the reporting calendar stay stable so that change can be seen; when the instrument changes, the change is versioned and the old results stay readable.\n\n2.  **Evidence before score.** A score of 3 needs an approved document and proof of use; interviews and FGDs alone cap at 2. The same rule applies to M&E claims: an output is "done" only with its means of verification.\n\n3.  **One register.** Findings from the IAT, AARs, exercises, audits, compliance reports and feedback go into one corrective and preventive action (CAPA) register, defined in Chapter 1 (section 9.5, GOV-10) and run under this chapter.\n\n4.  **Closure means effective.** An action is closed when the problem it addressed has not recurred in the next test, exercise or event, not when work has started.\n\n5.  **Separate the scorer from the validator.** Whoever scores an indicator does not validate it; MODE enforces this in the database (ADR-029).\n\n6.  **Privacy by design.** M&E and knowledge products use aggregate counts; personal data stays with the office that collected it, under the Data Privacy Act.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter sets how the City monitors and evaluates its DRRM plan and its DRRM institutions, how it learns from events and exercises, how it tracks corrective action to closure, how it meets its reporting duties, and how it keeps and shares DRRM knowledge and data safely.\n\nIntended results:\n\n1.  The LDRRMP has an approved indicator set with baselines, and the CDRRMC receives an annual results report each January (A10.4).\n\n2.  The IAT is applied every year with the same instrument, evidence rules and separation of duties, and its results feed the action plan and the Manual (MR-12 to MR-15).\n\n3.  Every EOC activation at Blue or Red, every State of Calamity or State of Imminent Disaster, and every exercise is followed by an after-action review whose findings are tracked (A10.6, B7.4).\n\n4.  Every corrective action in the CAPA register has an owner, a due date and verification evidence, and is closed only when shown to be effective.\n\n5.  The 45-day post-declaration report is submitted on time after each State of Imminent Disaster (A10.8), and all other mandated reports follow a calendar.\n\n6.  A disaster event and loss database holds the City''s history from 1991 onwards in a standard format (A5.4).\n\n7.  Lessons from Ormoc''s own events and from other LGUs and networks are recorded with the changes they produced (A10.9).\n\n8.  DRRM data handling meets the Data Privacy Act: a designated DPO, privacy notices, data-sharing agreements and a breach procedure (A5.5).\n\n# 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\n**In scope:** monitoring and evaluation of the LDRRMP and of the CDRRMO and EOC; the annual IAT cycle; after-action reviews and exercise evaluation; the CAPA register; compliance and post-declaration reporting; the disaster event and loss database; records, lessons and peer learning; protection of DRRM data; management review and performance reporting.\n\n**Table 7.4**\n\n*Subjects Handled in Other Chapters*\n\n| **Subject**                                                                               | **Where it is handled**                          |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| Definition of the CAPA register, governance KPIs (GOV-09) and records management (GOV-08) | Chapter 1 (sections 9.4 and 9.5; SOP register)   |\n| Situation reports during an event and their cut-off times                                 | Chapter 4 (SOP 4-08; PR-SITREP-CYCLE)            |\n| Hot wash and the after-action record at deactivation                                      | Chapter 4 (SOP 4-09; form ER-F06), received here |\n| Recovery monitoring and closeout                                                          | Chapter 5 (SOP 5-07; REC-F06, REC-F09)           |\n| Financial and utilisation reports                                                         | Chapter 6 (SOP 6-02; FIN-F03)                    |\n| Planning, conduct and scheduling of drills and exercises                                  | Chapter 9                                        |\n| Gawad KALASAG and SGLG submissions                                                        | Chapter 10                                       |\n\n**Institutional boundaries.** The CDRRMC monitors and evaluates the LDRRMP; this chapter equips it to do so but does not take that function over. Thematic focal offices report on their own PPAs. The CDRRMO Research and Planning Division is proposed as the M&E unit; it compiles, checks and analyses, but does not score its own work in the IAT. The City DPO decides privacy questions. Part C lead offices own their interface indicators.\n\n**Table 7.5**\n\n*Roles in Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning*\n\n| **Role**                                                | **Responsibilities**                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **CDRRMC**                                              | Approves the LDRRMP indicator set and targets; receives the annual results report, the IAT results and the quarterly CAPA status; decides corrective actions beyond the CDRRMO''s authority (RA 10121 Sec. 11(b); EO 2024-032A Sec. 2(a)). |\n| **CDRRMO Head**                                         | Accountable for this chapter; chairs the quarterly management review; approves AAR reports, Manual versions and the closure of High-priority CAPA entries.                                                                                |\n| **Research and Planning Division (M&E unit, proposed)** | Keeps the indicator set, data and reports; runs the IAT cycle logistics; keeps the CAPA register, the reporting calendar, the event and loss database and the lessons register.                                                           |\n| **CDRRMP Technical Working Group**                      | Thematic focal offices report physical and financial accomplishment of their PPAs (EO 2022-00020).                                                                                                                                        |\n| **Assessors and validators**                            | Assessors score with evidence; validators confirm or revise; a person never validates a score they set (ADR-029).                                                                                                                         |\n| **AAR facilitator**                                     | A trained person who did not hold a command role in the event, named for each AAR.                                                                                                                                                        |\n| **City Data Protection Officer**                        | Advises on privacy notices, data-sharing agreements, suppression of small counts and breach notification.                                                                                                                                 |\n| **City Records Officer**                                | Retention and disposal of DRRM records under the National Archives rules.                                                                                                                                                                 |\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\nChapter 1 sets the general legal basis. Table 7.6 lists the instruments that govern monitoring, evaluation, reporting and data in DRRM. The official text governs.\n\n**Table 7.6**\n\n*Instruments Governing Monitoring, Evaluation, Reporting and DRRM Data*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                                                  | **What it requires**                                                                                                                                                          | **How this chapter applies it**                                      |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA 10121 Sec. 11(b)                                                                                             | The LDRRMC approves, monitors and evaluates the LDRRMP and regularly reviews and tests the plan.                                                                              | SOP 7-01; annual results report                                      |\n| RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)                                                                                             | LDRRMO functions, including consolidating local disaster risk information, knowledge management and reporting (items cited by IAT v1.7 for A5.4 and A10.4).                   | SOPs 7-01, 7-07, 7-08                                                |\n| NDRRMP 2020–2030 and its Results-Based Monitoring and Evaluation Plan                                           | National outcomes and indicators; M&E matrices with indicators, baselines, targets, data sources and responsible offices; annual accomplishment reporting; Sendai monitoring. | Indicator overlay (section 6); ME-F01 matrix                         |\n| NDRRMC LDRRMP Formulation Guidebook (2022)                                                                      | Stage 5 of LDRRMP formulation: a results-based M&E plan.                                                                                                                      | Section 6; SOP 7-01                                                  |\n| NDRRMC Memo No. 147, s. 2017                                                                                    | Review of LDRRMPs and their consistency with the AIP.                                                                                                                         | SOP 7-01 step 5                                                      |\n| RA 12287 IRR Rule VI Sec. 1                                                                                     | Report within 45 days of lifting a State of Imminent Disaster: declaration status, AA fund use, estimated economic and social costs minimised.                                | SOP 7-06; GOV-F13; Annex 7-D                                         |\n| DILG Region VIII harmonized disaster response reporting template and SOP (RDRRMC VIII Res. No. 12-006, s. 2023) | Report types and cut-off times for LGU reports.                                                                                                                               | Reporting calendar (section 10); Chapter 4 runs the SitReps          |\n| RA 10173 (Data Privacy Act) and IRR                                                                             | Lawful processing, proportionality, security, data-sharing agreements, rights of data subjects, a DPO.                                                                        | Section 12; SOP 7-09                                                 |\n| NPC Circular No. 16-03                                                                                          | Personal data breach management, including notification of the NPC and affected data subjects within 72 hours of knowledge of a breach.                                       | SOP 7-09 steps 5–7                                                   |\n| NPC Circular No. 2023-06                                                                                        | Security of personal data in government and the private sector.                                                                                                               | SOP 7-09 step 3                                                      |\n| RA 9470 (National Archives of the Philippines Act)                                                              | Records management and disposal under approved retention schedules.                                                                                                           | Section 11.2                                                         |\n| Sendai Framework 2015–2030; UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard (Detailed Level, 2017)                          | Global targets A–G and their indicators; scorecard criteria; disaster loss data for monitoring.                                                                               | Indicator overlay; event and loss database fields (Annex 7-E); MR-18 |\n| Local: EO 2024-032A Sec. 2(a); EO 2022-00020; EO 2023-0026                                                      | CDRRMC M&E function; CDRRMP TWG and thematic focal offices; Project TRANSFORM TWG.                                                                                            | Roles (section 3); SOP 7-01                                          |\n\n> PENDING NATIONAL RULES. The NDRRMC monitoring and evaluation framework for anticipatory action under RA 12287 has not been issued. Until it is, the 45-day report follows the IRR text and GOV-F13; SOP 7-06 will be revised when the framework is issued.\n\n# 5. Systems Thinking and the Learning Cycle\n\nThe CDRRMO learns in two ways. In single-loop learning it corrects how a procedure was carried out: a SitRep was late, so the cut-off reminder is moved earlier. In double-loop learning it questions the procedure or its assumptions: SitReps are late because the same staff run the EOC and the field, so the roster changes (Argyris and Schön, 1978). The IAT and the AAR find problems of both kinds; the CAPA register records which kind each action is, so that repeated single-loop fixes to the same problem trigger a deeper review.\n\nFigure 7.2 shows the learning system. Evidence arrives from five sources: the annual IAT cycle, LDRRMP monitoring, after-action reviews and exercises, audits and compliance reports, and feedback and peer learning. One register turns it into change in plans, the Manual, MODE and people''s capacity. The next cycle measures again, which is how effectiveness is verified.\n\n**Figure 7.2**\n\n*The City DRRM Learning System*\n\n![Figure 2: 5. Systems Thinking and the Learning Cycle](/library/chp7v10/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from the PDCA cycle in Chapter 1 (section 9.2) and the CAPA requirements in Chapter 1 (section 9.5).\n\n-   **Against drifting goals.** Standards are set by the IAT rubric and the law, not by last year''s performance. The register shows Mandatory gaps first.\n\n-   **Against shifting the burden.** An AAR finding that recurs in two events is escalated from a single-loop fix to a CDRRMC decision on the fundamental solution (staffing, issuance, budget).\n\n-   **Delays are named.** Each action states when its effect can be seen (the next drill, the next season, the next IAT cycle), so the CDRRMC does not abandon a correct fix before it has had time to work.\n\n# 6. Monitoring and Evaluation of the LDRRMP\n\n## 6.1 The Integrated M&E Design\n\nThe City already adopted the design in its 10-Year Plan (Figure 7.3). This chapter does not replace it; it operates it. The UNDRR Ten Essentials are the base. The NDRRMP outcomes, the DILG capacity pillars, the Sendai targets and the Regional Development Plan of Region VIII are overlays that align local indicators with national and regional reporting. This Manual adds one layer: the IAT Institutional Maturity Index as the measure of the CDRRMO and the EOC themselves.\n\n**Figure 7.3**\n\n*Base and Overlays of the LDRRMP Results-Based M&E*\n\n![Figure 3: 6.1 The Integrated M&E Design](/library/chp7v10/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Table 33 (Integrated Criteria for Results-based Monitoring and Evaluation) of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032. Regional Development Plan indicators are marked "to be determined" in the plan.\n\n## 6.2 Indicator Set, Baselines and Targets\n\nEach indicator is recorded on the M&E matrix (ME-F01, aligned with the plan''s Form 5D): result level (output, outcome, impact), indicator and definition, unit, baseline value and year, target by year, data source, collection method and frequency, responsible office, and the Ten Essentials, NDRRMP, Sendai and IAT codes it maps to. Table 7.7 shows the levels. Targets are set by the CDRRMC; this chapter sets none.\n\n**Table 7.7**\n\n*Result Levels in the LDRRMP M&E Matrix*\n\n| **Level**         | **What is measured**                                                | **Examples of indicator types (targets set by the CDRRMC)**                                                                                                                                          |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Impact            | Change in disaster losses and resilience over the plan period       | Deaths, missing and affected persons per 100,000 (Sendai A, B); direct economic loss (Sendai C); damage to critical infrastructure and disruption of basic services (Sendai D); Ten Essentials score |\n| Outcome           | Change in institutions, systems and behaviour that the PPAs aim for | IAT IMI and Mandatory compliance; share of barangays with functional BDRRMCs; share of families reached by warnings who took protective action; share of recovery PPAs completed to BBB standards    |\n| Output            | Products and services delivered by the PPAs                         | Plans approved; drills held; people trained; early warning devices installed; evacuation centres upgraded; stocks at target                                                                          |\n| Input and process | Resources and how well they are used                                | LDRRMF utilisation (Chapter 6); staffing filled (Chapter 2); SitRep timeliness (Chapter 4)                                                                                                           |\n\n*Note.* Sendai indicators are reported as the City''s own figures for monitoring; they do not imply a national target allocation to the City.\n\n## 6.3 Reporting Products\n\n**Table 7.8**\n\n*LDRRMP M&E Reporting Products*\n\n| **Product**                        | **Timing**                          | **Content**                                                                                | **Recipient**                        |\n|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Annual progress and results report | Each January, for the previous year | ME-F01 values against baselines and targets; PPA status; IAT results; CAPA status; lessons | CDRRMC; LDC; posted on the City site |\n| Physical and financial report      | Each December                       | Physical accomplishment and LDRRMF use by PPA (with Chapter 6 FIN-F03)                     | CDRRMC; LDC                          |\n| Mid-term evaluation                | 2029                                | Progress at the midpoint; relevance of PPAs; revision of the plan                          | CDRRMC; SP                           |\n| End-of-plan evaluation             | 2032 (proposed)                     | Results against the plan''s outcomes; basis for the next LDRRMP                             | CDRRMC; SP                           |\n\n*Note.* The first three products are set by the 10-Year Plan; the end-of-plan evaluation is proposed by this chapter for CDRRMC decision.\n\n## 6.4 Data Quality\n\nEach value carries its source, date and the office that supplied it. A blank means "not reported" and is never entered as zero. Barangay figures use the 85-barangay PSGC list (Chapter 3). Values that change by more than half from the previous year are checked with the source office before publication. The M&E unit keeps the calculation for each composite indicator so that another person can repeat it.\n\n# 7. The Institutional Assessment Cycle\n\nThe IAT measures the CDRRMO and the EOC themselves. The Introduction (sections 5.1 to 5.3) describes the instrument and the baseline. This section sets how it is used every year.\n\n## 7.1 The Instrument\n\nIAT v1.7 has 140 indicators in 22 domains: Part A (CDRRMO), 82 indicators in 10 domains; Part B (EOC), 43 indicators in 7 domains; Part C (interface with other City offices and partners), 15 indicators in 5 domains. Parts A and B have 79 Mandatory and 46 Good Practice indicators and form the Institutional Maturity Index (IMI). Part C names a lead office other than the CDRRMO and is reported as a separate Interface Index, never added to the IMI. The 108-indicator sheet in the City''s earlier statement of design principles is IAT v1.0, which the Vault records as superseded.\n\n## 7.2 Scoring and Evidence Rules\n\n**Table 7.9**\n\n*IAT Scoring Rubric and Evidence Rule*\n\n| **Score** | **Level**      | **Meaning and minimum evidence**                                                                     |\n|-----------|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 0         | Absent         | Nothing exists.                                                                                      |\n| 1         | Initial        | Exists verbally or informally only.                                                                  |\n| 2         | Developing     | A draft or partial arrangement exists. The maximum for a score supported only by interviews or FGDs. |\n| 3         | Compliant      | An approved document exists **and** there is proof of use.                                           |\n| 4         | Advanced       | Level 3 plus a test, exercise or AAR showing improvement.                                            |\n| NA        | Not applicable | Only with a written justification.                                                                   |\n\n*Note.* From the IAT v1.7 Scoring_Lookups sheet and Methodology v1.1, section 5.2. Until validation, the lower score stands.\n\n**Table 7.10**\n\n*IAT Indices and Priority Formulas*\n\n| **Measure**                      | **Formula and rule**                                                                                                                                                                        |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Domain or part index**         | Σ(score × weight) ÷ Σ(weight) over scored items. Bands: below 0.5 Absent; 0.5–1.49 Initial; 1.5–2.49 Developing; 2.5–3.49 Compliant; 3.5 and above Advanced. The unrounded value is banded. |\n| **Institutional Maturity Index** | The index over all scored items in Parts A and B.                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Mandatory compliance**         | Scored Mandatory items at 3 or 4 ÷ scored Mandatory items. A domain is "Compliant" only at 100%.                                                                                            |\n| **Interface Index**              | The index over Part C, reported separately.                                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Gap priority**                 | (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 for Mandatory items; × 1.0 for Good Practice and all Part C items. High 9 and above; Medium 4 to 8.9; Low below 4.                                               |\n\n*Note.* The scoring rules are held once in SQL in MODE and mirrored in the application code under a parity test (ADR-005); a change to a formula is a change to the controlled instrument and needs a new IAT version.\n\n## 7.3 Validation and Separation of Duties\n\nEvery score is validated before the results are final. MODE records who set each score; a validation by the same person is refused. A validator either confirms the score or revises it; a revision needs a written note, and the submitted score is kept. Baseline rows loaded from the workbook have no recorded scorer, so the lead assessor does not validate them (ADR-029; Methodology v1.1, section 6.1). The agreement rate between scorers and validators is reported as a calibration signal, not as a performance measure of individuals.\n\n## 7.4 The Annual Cycle\n\nFigure 7.4 shows the cycle. MODE carries each cycle through five statuses: draft, fieldwork, validation, final and archived. A final cycle is not edited; corrections are made in the next cycle. The proposed timing places fieldwork and validation in the third and fourth quarters, so the results reach the January annual report and the budget call that follows.\n\n**Figure 7.4**\n\n*The Annual IAT Cycle*\n\n![Figure 4: 7.4 The Annual Cycle](/library/chp7v10/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from the MODE assessment_cycles statuses, ADR-005, ADR-029 and Methodology v1.1. The timing in the reporting calendar (Figure 7.6) is a proposal for CDRRMC decision.\n\n## 7.5 From Gap to Action to Manual\n\nEach gap routes to an improvement action with a responsible office, target date and budget source, and to the Manual section that addresses it (MR-14). MODE holds actions per indicator and cycle with a status (not started, ongoing, done, dropped) and shows the chain from indicator to master requirement to Manual document in the traceability view. Actions arising from the IAT are copied into the CAPA register so that one register holds all corrective work.\n\n> PLATFORM NOTE. The MODE actions table has no fields for root cause, verification evidence or effectiveness review, which Chapter 1 (Table 1.23) requires of a CAPA entry. Until they are added, the CAPA register is kept on ME-F05 and the MODE action carries the ME-F05 reference. The gap is listed in section 17.\n\n# 8. After-Action Reviews and Exercise Evaluation\n\nAn after-action review asks four questions: what was supposed to happen; what actually happened; why there was a difference; and what will be done about it. It examines systems and decisions, not individuals. Figure 7.5 shows the path from trigger to closure.\n\n**Table 7.11**\n\n*Events That Require an After-Action Review*\n\n| **Trigger**                                                          | **Minimum form**                                                                   | **Proposed timing**                                 |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|\n| EOC activation at Blue or Red                                        | Hot wash at deactivation (Chapter 4, SOP 4-09) and a full AAR (ME-F03)             | Hot wash on deactivation; AAR report within 30 days |\n| State of Calamity or State of Imminent Disaster                      | Full AAR; for a State of Imminent Disaster, linked to the 45-day report (SOP 7-06) | Within 30 days of lifting                           |\n| Functional or full-scale exercise                                    | Exercise evaluation (ME-F04)                                                       | Within 15 days of the exercise                      |\n| Tabletop exercise or drill                                           | Short evaluation (ME-F04, short form)                                              | Within 7 days                                       |\n| Near miss or significant procedural failure, even without activation | Focused AAR (ME-F03, focused form)                                                 | Within 15 days of discovery                         |\n\n*Note.* The timings are proposals of this chapter for CDRRMC adoption; they are not set by national rules.\n\n**Figure 7.5**\n\n*From After-Action Review to Verified Closure*\n\n![Figure 5: 8. After-Action Reviews and Exercise Evaluation](/library/chp7v10/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. ER-F06 is the Chapter 4 after-action and corrective action record; ME-F05 is the CAPA register entry.\n\n**Who takes part.** The EOC Manager, section chiefs, the Incident Commander or a representative, sector offices and barangays involved, and, where possible, community representatives and partners. A facilitator who did not hold a command role leads. Participants are told that the AAR is blame-free and that its record names positions, not people.\n\n**What is recorded.** The timeline from the official warning products and the logbook; decisions and their basis; what worked; what did not, with evidence; root or contributing causes; and recommended actions. Annex 7-C gives the report outline. Exercise evaluations follow the exercise objectives and the EOC Capability Assessment Checklist where it applies.\n\n# 9. Corrective and Preventive Action Management\n\nChapter 1 (section 9.5) defines the CAPA register and its fields; SOP GOV-10 names it. This chapter operates it (SOP 7-05). Every source in Figure 7.2 feeds it: IAT gaps, AAR and exercise findings, COA observations (Chapter 6, FIN-F11), compliance report gaps, recovery monitoring (Chapter 5), stakeholder feedback and changes in law.\n\n**Table 7.12**\n\n*CAPA Status Rules*\n\n| **Status**         | **Meaning**                                                                              | **Who may set it**                              |\n|--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| Open               | Entered with problem statement, source and risk consequence; owner and due date proposed | M&E unit                                        |\n| Planned            | Root cause, action, owner, due date and resources agreed                                 | Accountable owner, confirmed by the CDRRMO Head |\n| In progress        | Work has started                                                                         | Accountable owner                               |\n| Implemented        | Evidence of completion attached                                                          | Accountable owner                               |\n| Closed – effective | The problem has not recurred in the next test, exercise, event or IAT cycle              | CDRRMO Head; the CDRRMC for High-priority items |\n| Reopened           | The problem recurred; a new cause analysis is required                                   | M&E unit                                        |\n| Escalated          | Overdue twice, or beyond the CDRRMO''s authority                                          | CDRRMO Head to the CDRRMC                       |\n\n*Note.* An action is never closed because it has started (Chapter 1, section 9.5). Priority follows the IAT gap-priority bands where the source is an IAT indicator; otherwise the CDRRMO Head assigns High, Medium or Low by life-safety and legal consequence.\n\n# 10. Compliance Reporting and the Reporting Calendar\n\nThe City owes reports to several bodies. Missing one is a compliance gap; sending the same figures in different versions is a coherence gap. The M&E unit keeps one calendar (ME-F06) and one source for each figure (Figure 7.6; Table 7.13).\n\n**Table 7.13**\n\n*Mandated and Planned Reports*\n\n| **Report**                                                   | **Recipient**                                 | **Timing**                                    | **Owner and source**           |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| Situation reports (pre-, during and post-event; terminal)    | Province; OCD VIII and RDRRMC VIII; DILG VIII | Cut-off times of the DILG VIII harmonized SOP | EOC (Chapter 4, SOP 4-08)      |\n| DROMIC displacement reports                                  | DSWD Field Office VIII                        | Per DSWD schedule during events               | CSWDO                          |\n| 45-day post-declaration report under RA 12287                | OCD (route to be confirmed with OCD VIII)     | Within 45 days of lifting                     | CDRRMO Head; SOP 7-06; GOV-F13 |\n| LDRRMF utilisation report and Full Disclosure Policy posting | CDRRMC; public; DILG                          | Quarterly                                     | Chapter 6, SOP 6-02            |\n| Operation L!sto and other DILG preparedness reports          | DILG City Office and Region VIII              | Per DILG issuance                             | CDRRMO with DILG City Office   |\n| Annual LDRRMP results report                                 | CDRRMC; LDC                                   | January                                       | M&E unit; SOP 7-01             |\n| Physical and financial report of the LDRRMP                  | CDRRMC; LDC                                   | December                                      | M&E unit with Chapter 6        |\n| IAT results and action plan                                  | CDRRMC; CDRRMO staff                          | On cycle close                                | M&E unit; SOPs 7-02, 7-03      |\n| Gawad KALASAG and SGLG submissions                           | OCD; DILG                                     | Per annual guidelines                         | Chapter 10                     |\n\n*Note.* Report routes and schedules follow the issuing agency''s current rules; the M&E unit confirms them each January and updates ME-F06.\n\n**Figure 7.6**\n\n*Proposed Annual Reporting and Review Calendar*\n\n![Figure 6: 10. Compliance Reporting and the Reporting Calendar](/library/chp7v10/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. Orange marks the IAT cycle. Event-driven reports run on their own clocks and are not shown.\n\n# 11. Knowledge Management\n\n## 11.1 Disaster Event and Loss Database\n\nThe City needs one record of every disaster that affected it: what happened, where, to whom and at what cost (A5.4). The database uses the Sendai Framework and DesInventar fields (Annex 7-E), records barangay as the smallest unit using PSGC codes, and holds aggregate counts only. It starts with the events already documented (the 1991 flash flood, Yolanda in 2013, the 2017 earthquake, Urduja in 2017, Ursula in 2019 and Odette in 2021) and is updated after every event from the RDANA, DROMIC, PDNA and final SitRep. Each figure carries its source; implausible figures, such as the Yolanda damage printed in the 10-Year Plan, are flagged until verified (SOP 7-07).\n\n## 11.2 Records and the Vault\n\nDRRM records follow Chapter 1 (GOV-08). Issuances, plans, reports and evidence are kept in the MODE Vault; documents are superseded, never deleted, and each has an owner office and a date. Retention and disposal follow the schedules approved under RA 9470; nothing is disposed of by the CDRRMO on its own. Evidence files attached to IAT scores identify sources by office or position, never by an informant''s name (MR-15).\n\n## 11.3 Lessons Register and Manual Revision\n\nA lesson is recorded only when it changed something or when a change has been proposed. The lessons register (ME-F08) links each lesson to its source (AAR, exercise, IAT, peer exchange), the change made (plan, Manual section, form, training, MODE rule) and the CAPA entry. Changes to the Manual are made as new versions in the MODE Library; approved versions are never edited, and only the CDRRMO Head approves a version.\n\n## 11.4 Peer Learning\n\nOrmoc takes part in several networks: the Making Cities Resilient 2030 initiative, the National Resilience Council Resilient LGU program (SP Res. No. 2021-036), the EDC Adopt-a-City partnership and DENR–EDC Project TRANSFORM (EO 2023-0026), and OCD forums. Each exchange is recorded with what was learned and what the City changed as a result (A10.9). Partnerships themselves are managed under Chapter 8.\n\n# 12. Protection of DRRM Data\n\nDRRM work collects personal data: evacuee and beneficiary lists, casualty lists, contact rosters, volunteer records. The Data Privacy Act applies in full, including during emergencies. MODE is designed to hold aggregate counts only for affected populations; named lists stay with the office that collected them (CSWDO, CHO) and are shared only under a data-sharing agreement or as the law allows.\n\n**Table 7.14**\n\n*Data Protection Controls for DRRM Information*\n\n| **Control**             | **Requirement**                                                                                                                             | **Owner**                           |\n|-------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Data Protection Officer | The City DPO is designated and covers DRRM processing; a CDRRMO compliance officer for privacy is named.                                    | City Administrator; CDRRMO Head     |\n| Privacy notices         | Short notices on registration, evacuation and relief forms state purpose, sharing and retention.                                            | CSWDO; CDRRMO                       |\n| Data-sharing agreements | Signed with the CSWDO, CHO, CPDO and partners that exchange personal data with the CDRRMO.                                                  | City Legal Office; DPO              |\n| Aggregate rule          | Reports, SitReps, the event database and M&E products use counts; blanks are "not reported".                                                | M&E unit; EOC                       |\n| Small counts            | Where a count in a sex-, age- or disability-disaggregated table could identify a person, the DPO decides whether to suppress or combine it. | DPO                                 |\n| Security                | Role-based access; encryption in transit; access logs; shared-device sign-out (MODE ADR-004, ADR-014); NPC Circular No. 2023-06.            | City ICT office; platform custodian |\n| Breach response         | Contain, assess and, where required, notify the NPC and affected data subjects within 72 hours of knowledge (NPC Circular No. 16-03).       | DPO; CDRRMO Head                    |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The DPO designation, privacy notices and data-sharing agreements were not sighted in the document set (A5.5). The CPDO and the City Administrator''s Office are to confirm the current arrangements at the workshop.\n\n# 13. Management Review and Performance Reporting\n\nChapter 1 (section 9.4, GOV-09) sets governance KPIs. Each chapter of the Manual adds its own performance measures. The M&E unit consolidates them into one performance report for two reviews.\n\n**Table 7.15**\n\n*Management Reviews*\n\n| **Review**               | **Frequency**                                | **Inputs**                                                                                                   | **Outputs**                                                             |\n|--------------------------|----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRRMO management review | Quarterly                                    | Chapter KPIs; CAPA status; reporting calendar status; open AAR actions; stock and fund status from Chapter 6 | Decisions on overdue actions; items for the CDRRMC                      |\n| CDRRMC review            | Quarterly, with an annual session in January | Annual results report; IAT results; CAPA High-priority items; proposed Manual revisions                      | Resolutions on corrective actions, plan revisions and budget priorities |\n\n# 14. Standard Operating Procedures\n\nEach SOP has a control block and a step table. Every step states the actor (a position or office, never a person), the action and decision rule, inputs and outputs, and timing, control and escalation. Form codes are those of section 18. All MODE templates named here are proposals except where an existing template or form is cited; none of SOPs 7-01 to 7-09 is yet loaded in MODE.\n\n## SOP 7-01 \\| LDRRMP monitoring, annual results report and review\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning (M&E unit), with the CDRRMP Technical Working Group. **Purpose:** Measure the LDRRMP against its indicators each year and give the CDRRMC an evidence-based results report.\n\n**Table 7.16**\n\n*SOP 7-01 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                                                 |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP7-01 (proposed)                                                                                           |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMC approves the indicator set, targets and the annual report (RA 10121 Sec. 11(b)); LDC receives it                   |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                                                              |\n| **Trigger**                     | Approval of a new or revised LDRRMP (indicator set); each December (data call); each January (report)                     |\n| **Forms**                       | ME-F01; ME-F02                                                                                                            |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.4 (2, Medium); A3.1 (Chapter 3)                                                                                       |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-07; MR-11; MR-18                                                                                                       |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 10121 Sec. 11(b); NDRRMP 2020–2030 RBME Plan; NDRRMC LDRRMP Formulation Guidebook (2022); NDRRMC Memo No. 147, s. 2017 |\n| **Cross-references**            | Chapter 3 (LDRRMP); Chapter 6 (FIN-F03); SOPs 7-02, 7-05                                                                  |\n\n**Table 7.17**\n\n*SOP 7-01 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Set the matrix</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit with thematic focal offices</th>\n<th><p>Record each indicator on ME-F01 with definition, unit, baseline, target, source, frequency, responsible office and framework codes.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Every indicator has a baseline or a dated plan to establish one?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> LDRRMP; Form 5D</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F01 matrix</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 60 days of plan approval</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> CDRRMC approves the matrix</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Call for data</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Send the data call to focal offices with the ME-F01 extract for each.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Call sent to all focal offices?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> ME-F01</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Data call</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> First week of December</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Call logged</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Collect and check</th>\n<th>Focal offices; M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Offices submit values with means of verification; the M&amp;E unit checks source, date and arithmetic and queries large changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Value supported by a means of verification? If not, record "not verified".</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Office submissions</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Checked dataset; query log</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> By 31 December</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Blank means not reported, never zero</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Office head for missing data</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Analyse</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Compare values with baselines and targets; add IAT results (SOP 7-03), CAPA status (SOP 7-05) and financial data (Chapter 6).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Off-track results explained?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Checked dataset</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Draft ME-F02</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Second week of January</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Calculations kept for repetition</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Review consistency</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit with CPDO</th>\n<th><p>Check consistency of results and PPAs with the AIP and CDP (Memo No. 147, s. 2017).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Any PPA without an AIP line or vice versa?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> AIP; CDP</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Consistency note</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> With step 4</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Note attached to ME-F02</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CPDO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Present</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Present the annual report to the CDRRMC and the LDC.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Accepted? Decisions on off-track results?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> ME-F02</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> CDRRMC resolution; LDC minutes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> January meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Decisions entered as CAPA items</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Publish and file</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Post the summary report; file the full report and data in the Vault.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Posted?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Approved report</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Posting proof; Vault entry</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 15 days of approval</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> No personal data in published products</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** ME-F01 matrix; data call; checked dataset and query log; ME-F02 report; consistency note; CDRRMC resolution; posting proof.\n\n## SOP 7-02 \\| Annual institutional assessment cycle (IAT): planning, fieldwork and scoring\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning, with designated assessors. **Purpose:** Score all 140 IAT indicators each year with the same instrument and evidence rules, so that change in the CDRRMO and EOC can be measured.\n\n**Table 7.18**\n\n*SOP 7-02 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                      |\n|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | MODE Assess module: assessment cycle (status draft to fieldwork)               |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMO Head opens the cycle; the CDRRMC receives the results                   |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                   |\n| **Trigger**                     | Annual schedule (third quarter); or a CDRRMC request after a major change      |\n| **Forms**                       | MODE assessment responses and evidence; ME-F05 for gaps                        |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | All 140 indicators; A10.4 (2, Medium)                                          |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-12; MR-13; MR-14; MR-15                                                     |\n| **Policy basis**                | IAT v1.7 and Methodology v1.1; Traceability and Data Dictionary v1.2; RA 10173 |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOP 7-03; Introduction section 5                                               |\n\n**Table 7.19**\n\n*SOP 7-02 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Open the cycle</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Open the cycle in MODE with name, year, IAT version and period; confirm the instrument version.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Same instrument as last year? If changed, record the version change.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Previous cycle</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Cycle in draft</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> July</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Version recorded on the cycle</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Assign</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Assign assessors by domain and validators by domain, never the same person for the same indicator; name Part C lead-office focal persons.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Separation kept?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Staff list</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Assignment list</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> July</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> MODE enforces at validation</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Plan evidence</th>\n<th>Assessors</th>\n<th><p>List the means of verification for each indicator from the Data Dictionary; request documents from offices.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Request sent for every document?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Data Dictionary</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Evidence request log</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> August</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Requests logged</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Office heads</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Fieldwork</th>\n<th>Assessors</th>\n<th><p>Document review, key informant interviews, FGDs and observation; attach evidence with its source by office or position.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is the evidence attached, not just described?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Documents; interviews</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Evidence records in MODE</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> September–October</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> No informant names stored</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> M&amp;E unit</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Score</th>\n<th>Assessors</th>\n<th><p>Score 0–4 or NA by the rubric; apply the evidence gate (FGD or KII only: maximum 2); write the gap and recommended action.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Does the score match the strongest evidence?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Scored responses</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> By end of October</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> NA only with justification</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> M&amp;E unit</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Hand over to validation</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Move the cycle to validation; prepare the validation queue and printable sheets.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> All indicators scored or marked NA?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Scored responses</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Cycle in validation</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> November</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Completeness report</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Cycle record; assignment list; evidence request log; evidence records; scored responses with gaps and actions; completeness report.\n\n## SOP 7-03 \\| Validation of assessment scores and release of results\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with validators and the M&E unit. **Purpose:** Confirm or revise every score through people who did not set it, and release the IMI, compliance and gap priorities as final.\n\n**Table 7.20**\n\n*SOP 7-03 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                               |\n|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | MODE Assess module: validation workshop screens (v_validation_progress) |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMO Head finalises the cycle; CDRRMC receives the results            |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | M&E unit; validators                                                    |\n| **Trigger**                     | Cycle moved to validation (SOP 7-02 step 6)                             |\n| **Forms**                       | MODE validation records; printable validation sheets; ME-F05            |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | All 140 indicators                                                      |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-12; MR-13; MR-14                                                     |\n| **Policy basis**                | ADR-029; Methodology v1.1, section 6; IAT v1.7                          |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 7-02, 7-05; Introduction section 5.3                               |\n\n**Table 7.21**\n\n*SOP 7-03 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Prepare</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Brief validators on the rubric and evidence rule; issue the queue by domain.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Each validator has no indicator they scored?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Queue</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Briefing record</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before the workshop</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> MODE refuses self-validation</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Validate</th>\n<th>Validators</th>\n<th><p>Review evidence; confirm the score or revise it with a written note.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Evidence supports the score? A revision needs a note.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Scored responses; evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Confirmed or revised responses</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Workshop days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Submitted score kept on revision</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Lead validator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Resolve disputes</th>\n<th>Lead validator with the domain owner</th>\n<th><p>Settle disagreements on the evidence; record the reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Agreement reached? If not, the lower score stands.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Disputed items</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Resolution notes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> During the workshop</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Reason recorded</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Compute</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Let MODE compute the domain indices, IMI, Mandatory compliance, Interface Index and gap priorities.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Results match an independent recomputation from the export?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Validated responses</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Results set</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> After validation</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Second person recomputes</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Platform custodian for a mismatch</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Finalise</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Move the cycle to final; archive the previous cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> All items validated?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Results set</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Final cycle</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 10 days of the workshop</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Final cycles are not edited</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Act and report</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Copy High and Medium gaps into the CAPA register; report results to the CDRRMC and staff.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Every High gap has a CAPA entry?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Final results</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 entries; results report</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Next CDRRMC meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Agreement rate reported as calibration only</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Briefing record; validation records with notes; dispute resolutions; recomputation check; final cycle; CAPA entries; results report.\n\n## SOP 7-04 \\| After-action review and exercise evaluation\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with a designated AAR facilitator. **Purpose:** Learn from every significant event and exercise, and turn findings into tracked corrective actions.\n\n**Table 7.22**\n\n*SOP 7-04 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Protocol: PR-AAR (proposed)                                                                             |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMO Head approves the AAR report; CDRRMC decides actions beyond the CDRRMO''s authority               |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Head; AAR facilitator                                                                            |\n| **Trigger**                     | Any trigger in section 8 (Table 7.11); hot wash and ER-F06 from Chapter 4, SOP 4-09                     |\n| **Forms**                       | ER-F06 (input); ME-F03; ME-F04; ME-F05                                                                  |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.6 (2, Medium); B7.4 (2, Medium); B7.3 (2, Medium)                                                   |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-11; MR-04; MR-05                                                                                     |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 10121 Sec. 11(b); NDRRMC Memo No. 131, s. 2018 (EOC management); EOC Capability Assessment Checklist |\n| **Cross-references**            | Chapter 4 SOP 4-09; Chapter 9 (exercises); SOP 7-05                                                     |\n\n**Table 7.23**\n\n*SOP 7-04 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Register the trigger</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Record the event or exercise and the AAR due date.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Trigger in section 8?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Deactivation record; exercise plan</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> AAR register entry</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 1 day</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Due date set</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Name the facilitator</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Name a facilitator who did not hold a command role; set participants.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Facilitator independent of the event?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Register entry</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Facilitator and participant list</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 3 days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Partners and barangays invited where involved</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Reconstruct</th>\n<th>Facilitator with M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Build the timeline from warning products, the logbook, SitReps and resource requests.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Each entry sourced?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> MODE logbook; SitReps; ER-F06</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Timeline</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before the session</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Official product IDs cited</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Hold the session</th>\n<th>Facilitator</th>\n<th><p>Ask the four questions; record what worked, what did not and why; keep the discussion blame-free.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Causes identified, not only symptoms?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Timeline</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Session notes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within the timing in section 8</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Positions, not names, in the record</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Write the report</th>\n<th>Facilitator</th>\n<th><p>Write ME-F03 (or ME-F04 for exercises): findings with evidence, causes, recommended actions, owners.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Each finding has an action?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Notes</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F03 or ME-F04 draft</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 10 days of the session</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Template of Annex 7-C</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Approve</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Approve the report; send items beyond the CDRRMO''s authority to the CDRRMC.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Approved?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft report</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Approved report</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within the AAR due date</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Filed in the Vault</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Register actions</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Enter each action in the CAPA register; mark recurring findings for double-loop review.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Finding seen before? Then escalate.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Approved report</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 entries</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 5 days of approval</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Link to the report</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** AAR register; participant list; timeline; session notes; ME-F03 or ME-F04; CAPA entries.\n\n## SOP 7-05 \\| Corrective and preventive action management (CAPA register)\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning (register keeper); each accountable owner. **Purpose:** Track every finding from any source to verified, effective closure (operates GOV-10).\n\n**Table 7.24**\n\n*SOP 7-05 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                     |\n|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP7-05 (proposed); MODE actions for IAT-sourced items           |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMO Head closes; CDRRMC closes High-priority items and decides escalations |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                  |\n| **Trigger**                     | A new finding from any source in Figure 7.2                                   |\n| **Forms**                       | ME-F05; ER-F06 and FIN-F11 as inputs                                          |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.6 (2, Medium); B7.4 (2, Medium); all IAT gaps                             |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-11; MR-14                                                                  |\n| **Policy basis**                | Chapter 1, section 9.5 (Table 1.23); RA 10121 Sec. 11(b)                      |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 7-01, 7-03, 7-04; Chapter 6 SOP 6-10; Chapter 5 SOP 5-07                 |\n\n**Table 7.25**\n\n*SOP 7-05 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Enter</th>\n<th>Register keeper</th>\n<th><p>Enter source, problem statement and risk consequence; assign priority.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Duplicate of an open item? Then link instead.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Finding</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (Open)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 5 working days of the finding</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Source document linked</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Analyse and plan</th>\n<th>Accountable owner</th>\n<th><p>State the root cause, action, responsible actors, target date and resources; mark single- or double-loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Cause supported by evidence?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> ME-F05</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (Planned)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 15 working days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> CDRRMO Head confirms the plan</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Implement</th>\n<th>Responsible actors</th>\n<th><p>Carry out the action; update status.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> On schedule?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Plan</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (In progress)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> By the target date</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Status updated at least monthly</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Accountable owner</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Verify</th>\n<th>Register keeper</th>\n<th><p>Check evidence of completion.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Evidence complete?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (Implemented)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On notice of completion</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Evidence filed in the Vault</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Review effectiveness</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head (CDRRMC for High)</th>\n<th><p>Check at the next test, exercise, event or IAT cycle whether the problem recurred.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Effective? If not, reopen with a new cause analysis.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Next test result</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (Closed – effective or Reopened)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> At the next test</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Closure only with effectiveness evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Report</th>\n<th>Register keeper</th>\n<th><p>Report status, overdue items and recurring findings to the quarterly reviews.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Overdue twice? Escalate.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Register</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> CAPA status report</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Quarterly</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Escalations recorded</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** ME-F05 register with status history; evidence of completion; effectiveness reviews; quarterly status reports.\n\n## SOP 7-06 \\| Post-declaration (45-day) report after a State of Imminent Disaster\n\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch7v1_v1.0.md (Chapter 7, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft')
